6.1.1 Path-dependent payoffs
Monte Carlo handles path-dependence naturally. For an average-strike Asian option, \(\Pi_0 = e^{-rT}\mathbb E^Q\bigl[\bigl(S_T - \tfrac1T\int_0^TS_u\,du\bigr)^+\bigr]\), which has no closed form in the BSM model:

discretise \([0, T]\) with \(t_j = jT/N\), \(j = 0, \dots, N\);
simulate \(M\) discretised paths \((\hat S^k_{t_j})_{j=0,\dots,N}\);
approximate each payoff by \(\bigl(\hat S^k_T - \tfrac1N\sum_{j=1}^N\hat S^k_{t_j}\bigr)^+\) and average.

In general, with \(g(S_t; t \leq T)\) the exact (possibly path-dependent) payoff, \(\hat S\) the discretised process and \(\hat S^k\) its \(k\)-th simulated path, \[\Pi_0(g) = e^{-rT}\mathbb E^Q[g(S)] \approx e^{-rT}\frac1M\sum_{k=1}^Mg(\hat S^k).\]

equity indices show a downward-sloping skew: the left tail is heavier and the right tail lighter (crash risk, “crashophobia” after 1987, and the leverage effect: volatility rises when prices fall);
currencies show a more symmetric smile: both tails heavier than log-normal;
jumps in the underlying create a pronounced smile at short maturities that flattens with maturity, whereas stochastic volatility creates smiles that build up over longer horizons;
the term structure of at-the-money volatility tends to be upward-sloping when volatility is historically low, and downward-sloping when it is high (mean reversion of volatility).

8.2 Zero-coupon bonds and forward rates
The fundamental purpose of the risk-free rate is to measure the value of time. Let \(P_t(T)\) be the value at \(t\) of one unit of currency delivered at \(T \geq t\) without risk — the price of a zero-coupon bond (ZCB), with \(P_T(T) = 1\). The term structure at \(t\) is the curve \(T \mapsto P_t(T)\); modelling it means describing its random evolution over time.

For \(t \leq S &lt; T\):

simple spot rate for \([S, T]\): \(\displaystyle L_S(S, T) = \frac{1}{T - S}\Bigl(\frac{1}{P_S(T)} - 1\Bigr)\), the rate for lending at \(S\) until \(T\);
simple forward rate for \([S, T]\) contracted at \(t\): \(\displaystyle L_t(S, T) = \frac{1}{T - S}\Bigl(\frac{P_t(S)}{P_t(T)} - 1\Bigr)\);
continuously compounded forward rate: \(\displaystyle F_t(S, T) = -\frac{\log P_t(T) - \log P_t(S)}{T - S}\);
instantaneous forward rate: \(\displaystyle f_t(T) = -\frac{\partial}{\partial T}\log P_t(T) = \lim_{S\to T}F_t(S, T)\), the rate contracted at \(t\) for \([T, T + dT]\);
short rate: \(r_t = f_t(t)\), the rate for \([t, t + dt]\).


Consequently \(P_t(T) = \exp\bigl(-\int_t^Tf_t(u)\,du\bigr)\).
Why the forward rate formula. At \(t\), buy \(P_t(S)/P_t(T)\) ZCBs maturing at \(T\) and sell one ZCB maturing at \(S\): zero cost. At \(S\) pay \(1\); at \(T\) receive \(P_t(S)/P_t(T)\). The rate for \([S, T]\) has been locked in at \(t\): \(1 + (T - S)L_t(S, T) = P_t(S)/P_t(T)\).
The bank account pays the short rate continuously: \(dB_t = r_tB_t\,dt\), \(B_0 = 1\), i.e. \(B_t = \exp\bigl(\int_0^tr_u\,du\bigr)\) — the value of rolling over, at each instant, an investment in ZCBs maturing at \(t + dt\). It is locally riskless. One can model the ZCB prices \(P_t(T)\), the forward rates \(f_t(T)\) (the Heath–Jarrow–Morton approach), the simple forward rates \(L_t(S, T)\) (LIBOR market models), or the short rate \(r_t\) and deduce everything else by no arbitrage. This chapter follows the last route.

9.6 Risk-free rates in insurance: Solvency II
Under Solvency II, EU insurers compute their solvency ratio from a market-value balance sheet, valuing liabilities (technical provisions) and the Solvency Capital Requirement with a regulatory risk-free curve published by EIOPA:

collect par rates of IRS (Euribor) or OIS (SARON, SONIA, SOFR, …) for the liquid maturities (up to about 20 years for the euro);
subtract a credit risk adjustment (10 bp for Euribor-based curves, zero for RFR-based ones);
bootstrap zero rates from the adjusted par rates;
extrapolate beyond the last liquid point up to 150 years with the Smith–Wilson method, converging to an ultimate forward rate;
optionally add a volatility adjustment or matching adjustment, which reflect the spread insurers earn on their assets.

Economic scenario generators. Life liabilities contain financial options and guarantees, profit-sharing rules, management actions and policyholder behaviour, so their best estimate (BEL) is computed by Monte Carlo projection of the balance sheet in an asset–liability model, over thousands of risk-neutral scenarios that start from the Solvency II curve. Typical rate models are the displaced LIBOR market model with stochastic volatility, the two-factor Hull–White model and the displaced Black–Karasinski model, calibrated to OIS (level) and swaptions (implied volatility). The IBOR→RFR transition did not change these models: OIS and OIS swaptions replace IRS and IRS swaptions, with small adjustments to conventions.
Measuring interest rate risk. Since cash flows of both sides depend on rates, the relevant sensitivity is the effective duration, computed by revaluation under parallel shifts \(\pm\Delta R\) of the curve: \[D^{ef}_A = -\frac{1}{A}\frac{\partial A}{\partial R} \approx -\frac{A^+ - A^-}{2A\,\Delta R}, \qquad D^{ef}_L = -\frac{1}{L}\frac{\partial L}{\partial R} \approx -\frac{L^+ - L^-}{2L\,\Delta R}.\] Duration matching chooses the asset portfolio so that the value-weighted durations coincide, minimising the duration gap \[D_{Gap} = D^{ef}_A - \frac LA\,D^{ef}_L,\] so that a parallel move of rates leaves the net asset value \(A - L\) approximately unchanged. Interest rate swaps and swaptions are the main hedging instruments.

10.3 Anatomy of a market-making system
Clients reach the bank through multi-dealer platforms (Bloomberg, Tradeweb, Digital Vega) or the bank’s own single-dealer platform, via two channels: request for quote (RFQ) and continuous price streaming.

Figure: the front-end handles connectivity, market data and routing; the back-end prices; requests outside automatic limits go to a trader.

The front-end handles connectivity to external platforms, receives price requests, sources market information and passes it, with the desk’s settings, to the back-end; it displays results and books trades.
The back-end contains the pricing logic for a collection of deals (a structure) and returns the results. It is usually written in C++ or Java.

The back-end performs four steps:

Valuation: the mid price of the structure and its Greeks (Delta, vega, rho, …);
Spreading: bid and offer obtained by subtracting and adding a symmetric spread to the mid;
Skewing: shifting the quote towards the side that reduces the book’s risk;
Hedging: an FX option is always booked Delta-hedged — the Delta is either exchanged with the client or executed in the spot market.

10.5 Spreading
Spreading decides how much to charge on top of the mid: too much and nobody trades; too little and the margin does not cover hedging costs and adverse moves. In practice:

the desk maintains a base ATM volatility spread term structure for each currency pair (a value per tenor);
multipliers widen or tighten it depending on market conditions (volatile periods, events), the instrument (strike, Delta, exotic features) and the counterparty (client tiering);
the spread includes the cost of hedging the trade (e.g. the bid–offer of the spot hedge);
for a structure of several deals, spreading accounts for the netting of risk between legs.

Since prices are quoted in vols, a spread of \(\delta\sigma\) vol points translates into premium through the vega: \(\text{premium spread} \approx \mathcal V\,\delta\sigma\).

Spot \(S = 1.10\), \(r_{USD} = 4\%\), \(r_{EUR} = 2.5\%\), 6-month at-the-money-forward call (\(K = F = 1.1083\)), mid vol \(8\%\). Garman–Kohlhagen gives a mid premium of \(0.02451\) USD per EUR, spot Delta \(0.505\) and vega \(0.00306\) USD per EUR per vol point. On a 10m EUR notional: mid premium \(245\,127\) USD, vega \(30\,633\) USD per vol point. Quoting \(7.85\%\) / \(8.15\%\) (a spread of \(0.15\) vol on each side) gives bid and offer premiums of \(240\,532\) and \(249\,722\) USD: about \(4\,600\) USD of margin per side. If the client buys, the bank books the option together with a Delta hedge: buying about 5.05m EUR spot.
